---
/**
 * /tools/ — the indexed tools hub.
 *
 * ── WHAT THIS PAGE WAS, AND WHY IT CHANGED ────────────────────────────────
 * Written when every child route was a noindexed "coming soon" placeholder, it
 * listed five things of which two were real. Three tools have since been built
 * and the hub had never been told. It now lists what exists, and replaces the
 * three placeholder listings — and the three placeholder pages, deleted this
 * round — with one section saying what this site does not publish and the
 * measurement behind each refusal.
 *
 * ── THE ONE RULE ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 * Every description is written from the BUILT PAGE, and every card carries the
 * refusal its tool makes. A hub that describes only capabilities re-promises
 * what each page declines, one click before the reader gets to the page that
 * declines it.
 */
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import Breadcrumbs from "../../components/Breadcrumbs.astro";
import { NOT_PUBLISHED, WORKING_TOOLS } from "../../data/toolsHub";

const title = "Texas homeowner tools — and what each one will not tell you | Texas Home Intelligence";
const description =
  "Three working tools and the ZIP dashboard for Austin and San Antonio: plumbing triage, " +
  "cooling load and warranty terms, hail and re-roofing on the public record. Each says what it " +
  "cannot see. Plus what this site does not publish, and the measurement behind each.";
---

<Base title={title} description={description}>
  <Breadcrumbs crumbs={[{ name: "Home", url: "/" }, { name: "Tools", url: "/tools/" }]} />

  <section class="hero">
    <div class="wrap" style="max-width:820px;">
      <span class="eyebrow">TOOLS</span>
      <h1>What we have built, and what we will not publish</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        Four things you can use now, each reading from the same public data as the rest of the
        site and each carrying its sources and dates. None of them asks for an address, and none
        of them has seen your house — so every one states what it cannot tell you, and that
        statement is on the card before you open it.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="block" id="working">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="grid-3">
        {WORKING_TOOLS.map((tool) => (
          <div class="card tool-card">
            <h2 class="tool-name">{tool.name}</h2>
            <p class="tool-route"><code>{tool.href}</code></p>
            <p>{tool.description}</p>
            {/*
              The refusal, at the same weight as the description and inside the
              same card. Not a footnote and not smaller type: it is half of what
              a reader needs to decide whether this page is the one they want.
            */}
            <p class="tool-wont"><strong>What it will not do.</strong> {tool.wontDo}</p>
            <p class="tool-cta"><a href={tool.href}>Open {tool.name} &rarr;</a></p>
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="block alt" id="not-published">
    <div class="wrap" style="max-width:820px;">
      <h2>What we do not publish, and why</h2>
      <p>
        Each of these was tried and stopped by something we measured rather than assumed. They are
        listed here rather than as pages promising them, because a page that says "coming soon"
        about a thing blocked on a source that may not exist is a promise we would be making with
        someone else's data.
      </p>
      <ul class="key-findings">
        {NOT_PUBLISHED.map((n) => (
          <li><strong>{n.heading}.</strong> {n.body}</li>
        ))}
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="block" id="elsewhere">
    <div class="wrap" style="max-width:820px;">
      <h2>Also on the site</h2>
      <p>
        The <a href="/data/">data catalog</a> lists every feed behind these tools, with its
        publisher, its update cadence and its limitations, and
        <a href="/methodology/">methodology</a> explains how a reading becomes a score. The
        QuoteReady project brief at <a href="/start/">/start/</a> is an intake form that collects
        a name, an email and a property address; it is not part of the current build and is not
        listed above with the tools, because those four ask for nothing.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>

<style>
  .tool-card { display: flex; flex-flow: column nowrap; gap: 10px; }
  .tool-name { font-size: 1.15rem; margin: 0; }
  .tool-route { margin: 0; }
  .tool-route code {
    font-family: var(--thi-font-mono); font-size: var(--thi-fs-caption);
    color: var(--ink-soft); overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }
  /* The refusal sits on a hairline rather than in a tint: BRAND.md keeps status
     colour for status, and "what this will not do" is not a warning state. */
  .tool-wont {
    margin: 4px 0 0; padding-left: 12px; border-left: 2px solid var(--line);
    color: var(--ink-soft); font-size: var(--thi-fs-body-sm);
  }
  .tool-wont strong { color: var(--ink); }
  /*
    A card CTA is a STANDALONE target, so WCAG 2.2 SC 2.5.8's inline exception
    — the one that covers the citation links elsewhere on the site — does not
    apply to it. As an inline link in a paragraph it measured 19px; a flex
    with a min-height makes the whole thing the target rather than the text
    box, without changing the type scale.
  */
  .tool-cta { margin: auto 0 0; font-weight: 700; }
  .tool-cta a {
    /* Block-level flex rather than the inline variant of it. The inline one is
       the obvious choice here, and its hyphenated name is also a Tailwind
       utility this bundle does not already carry — so writing it, even inside
       this comment, adds that utility to the SITEWIDE stylesheet and rehashes
       every page. Measured twice this round; Round 25's mechanism, and the
       name is deliberately not spelled out here. Block-level costs nothing:
       the CTA is the last line of a flex column, and spanning the card makes
       the target larger rather than worse. */
    display: flex; align-items: center; min-height: 44px; padding: 4px 0;
  }
</style>
